+ * (DP-2 3840x2160+0+0, HDMI-A-2 1920x1080+3840+0) DisplayWidth()/DisplayHeight() is
+ * the UNION of the two screens, i.e. 5760x2160. The fullscreen pin and the
+ * presentation rect used that union, so the picture straddled the monitor boundary:
+ * in the screenshot horror/Screenshot_20261101_003320.png the non-black content runs
+ * from x=1440 to x=5757, half on the 4K and half on the 1080p, while input referred
+ * only to the 4K. Rule: one monitor, the one with the largest overlap with the X
+ * window; on a tie, or when the window overlaps nothing, the primary monitor.
+ * WINE_NV_FSHACK_MONITOR=primary forces the primary either way.
+ *
+ * Returns a rect in root coordinates. With w==0 it returns the last valid choice (the
+ * input and clip paths do not have the window at hand), else the primary, else the
+ * screen union.
+ */
+
+RECT nv_fshack_monitor_current( Display *dpy, Window w )
+{
+    static RECT chosen = {0};
+    RECT nv_wnd = {0, 0, 0, 0};
+    RECT candidates[16];
+    RECT primary_rect = {0, 0, 0, 0};
+    int i, n;
+    const char *force = getenv( "WINE_NV_FSHACK_MONITOR" );
